For real elapsed timing, subtract two steady_clock::now() time points and convert the resulting duration. A monotonic clock is suited to elapsed time, but workload timings still vary; this deterministic example converts a supplied duration instead of promising a benchmark result.

steady_clock::now() takes no arguments and returns a time point from that clock. This separate complete example takes two readings and subtracts the earlier one from the later one:
```cpp
#include <chrono>
#include <iostream>
int main() {
    auto start = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    auto finish = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    auto elapsed = finish - start;
    std::cout << std::boolalpha
              << (elapsed >= std::chrono::steady_clock::duration::zero())
              << "\n";
}
```
Output: true. duration::zero() supplies a zero duration for comparison. The readings may be equal, so zero elapsed time is allowed; no exact duration or speed is promised.

