A failed save should show the failure and preserve the previous confirmed saved state. Here is a browser JavaScript fragment for a running same-origin API with GET/POST /entries and a verified session. Pass it an existing output element; this fragment is not a standalone server or sign-in system.
```javascript
async function saveMinutes(minutes, output) {
  const response = await fetch('/entries', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ minutes })
  });
  if (!response.ok) {
    const failure = await response.json();
    output.textContent = failure.error || 'Save failed.';
    return;
  }
  const read = await fetch('/entries');
  if (!read.ok) {
    output.textContent = 'Saved, but reload failed. Try loading again.';
    return;
  }
  output.textContent = JSON.stringify(await read.json(), null, 2);
}
```
response.ok means an HTTP success status. Only a successful write leads to a new read here; textContent displays returned text safely. A real interface must also catch rejected fetch promises, such as network failures.

