Return a new state object so the caller can retain the previous snapshot.

Here is a separate two-ticket example with a ten-unit window:
```javascript
function takeTicket(state, now) {
  const next = now >= state.start + 10
    ? {start: now, used: 0} : {...state};
  if (next.used >= 2) return {allowed: false, state: next};
  next.used += 1;
  return {allowed: true, state: next};
}
const full = {start: 0, used: 2};
console.log(takeTicket(full, 9));
console.log(takeTicket(full, 10));
console.log(full.used);
```
At time 9 the result is allowed:false with start:0 and used:2. At time 10 it is allowed:true with start:10 and used:1. The last output is 2: full was not changed. state supplies the old count and starting time; now supplies the current time in the same unit. The first line resets an expired window or copies the current one. The early return rejects a full window; otherwise one ticket is counted in the returned state. This calculates a decision only; it neither waits nor limits a real server.

This browser helper models one exact strong-tag comparison. For example, the text '"v2"' includes quotation marks as part of the HTTP tag. Real HTTP validator parsing has additional rules outside this small exercise.

This complete JavaScript model compares two public-card versions:
```javascript
function cardReply(current, received) {
  if (received === current) return {status: 304, body: null, etag: current};
  return {status: 200, body: {title: "Public card"}, etag: current};
}
console.log(cardReply('"card-3"', '"card-3"'));
console.log(cardReply('"card-3"', '"card-2"'));
```
The first result has status 304 and body:null; the second has status 200 and the Public card body. Both include etag with the text "card-3", including its quote characters. current is the server’s present tag; received is the client’s saved tag. The strict comparison takes the no-body branch only when they match. Returning an object does not send HTTP; an adapter must translate it into the real response.

In this simplified report, compare selfMs values. A frequently called function may still use less total self time than a slower function called once.

To find the largest measurement, start with the first row as the current best and visit the remaining rows. Replace the best only when the new selfMs is strictly greater, not equal; that keeps the first row on a tie. With measurements 2, 9, 9, the saved best changes to the first 9 and stays there. Check for an empty list before reading its first row.

This separate example selects the heaviest parcel rather than a profiler row:
```javascript
function heaviest(parcels) {
  if (parcels.length === 0) return null;
  let best = parcels[0];
  for (const parcel of parcels) {
    if (parcel.grams > best.grams) best = parcel;
  }
  return best.label;
}
console.log(heaviest([{label: "A", grams: 3}, {label: "B", grams: 8}, {label: "C", grams: 8}]));
console.log(heaviest([]));
```
The outputs are B and null. parcels is the input array. The empty-array guard runs before reading its first entry. best begins as A, becomes B when 8 exceeds 3, and stays B when C only ties. return sends back the chosen label. Assigning best selects a record without changing the array or records. The same comparison pattern can inspect measurements; this example itself performs no profiling.

